1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,660
We trained a small language model from scratch for Track

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:06,300
01 of the Global Innovation Build Challenge.

3
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:14,750
The rules cap it at fifty million trainable parameters, and ours has 49,822,228.

4
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:20,240
It learned from random weights on 20 billion tokens of openly licensed English

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:24,675
text, with our own tokenizer, on one H100 in about eight hours.

6
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:31,254
The model is a 14-layer transformer, 512 wide, with grouped-query attention,

7
00:00:31,254 --> 00:00:36,480
rotary position embeddings and QK-norm, and a squared ReLU in the MLP.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:40,738
U-net style skips connect early layers to late ones, and a value

9
00:00:40,738 --> 00:00:44,530
residual lets every layer reuse the first layer's values.

10
00:00:44,530 --> 00:00:50,030
The 32k embedding is tied to the output head and takes a third of the budget.

11
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:58,680
Counted once, as the one tensor the optimizer updates, the total is 49,822,228.

12
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:02,355
Counted twice, it is 66.6 million.

13
00:01:02,355 --> 00:01:04,280
We report both.

14
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:09,530
Training follows the modded-nanogpt speedrun recipe.

15
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:13,287
Muon updates the matrices inside the blocks, and AdamW

16
00:01:13,287 --> 00:01:15,930
updates the embedding and the scalars.

17
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:21,255
The learning rate warms up, holds, then decays to zero over the last 35 percent of

18
00:01:21,255 --> 00:01:25,930
training, and at that point the data switches to higher quality sources.

19
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:29,505
We also tried averaging the last few snapshots.

20
00:01:29,505 --> 00:01:33,255
It made no difference, so we report the final weights.

21
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:38,615
The data is FineWeb-Edu, DCLM, FineMath, FinePDFs and

22
00:01:38,615 --> 00:01:42,130
Wikipedia, all public and openly licensed.

23
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:47,955
WikiText-103 is built from Wikipedia, which is copied all over the web.

24
00:01:47,955 --> 00:01:51,726
Before filtering, about five percent of its test 13-grams

25
00:01:51,726 --> 00:01:54,505
appeared in a sample of our training data.

26
00:01:54,505 --> 00:02:00,623
So we drop every document sharing any 13-gram with WikiText validation or

27
00:02:00,623 --> 00:02:06,155
test, 4,969 documents in all, plus 16 Wikipedia articles by title.

28
00:02:06,155 --> 00:02:09,398
The multiple-choice benchmarks are not filtered, but

29
00:02:09,398 --> 00:02:12,205
their measured overlap is a handful of items.

30
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:17,157
Before spending eight GPU-hours on a main run, we ran twelve

31
00:02:17,157 --> 00:02:20,840
one-billion-token experiments, each changing one thing.

32
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:24,290
Muon beat AdamW by a wide margin.

33
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:29,349
The value residual gave the biggest perplexity gain, 57.2 down

34
00:02:29,349 --> 00:02:33,265
to 54.0, for 13 extra parameters, so we kept it.

35
00:02:33,265 --> 00:02:36,822
The quality anneal raised ARC-Easy but lowered PIQA,

36
00:02:36,822 --> 00:02:39,490
so our two main runs try two strengths.

37
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:43,609
Each arm is one seed, so benchmark gaps under about two points

38
00:02:43,609 --> 00:02:46,865
are noise, and we leaned on perplexity to decide.

39
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:51,093
Here are the final numbers for the submitted model,

40
00:02:51,093 --> 00:02:54,730
main_vres_anneal, trained on the full 20 billion tokens.

41
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:58,230
HellaSwag, 31.87 percent.

42
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:01,730
ARC-Easy, 55.18 percent.

43
00:03:01,730 --> 00:03:05,080
PIQA, 61.92 percent.

44
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:08,580
WinoGrande, 51.62 percent.

45
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:13,330
WikiText-103 perplexity, 39.42.

46
00:03:13,330 --> 00:03:17,934
Against the one-billion-token sweep run, ARC-Easy is up eight

47
00:03:17,934 --> 00:03:21,255
points and perplexity is down from 61 to 39.

48
00:03:21,255 --> 00:03:25,512
The PIQA drop the strong anneal showed at one billion tokens did not

49
00:03:25,512 --> 00:03:29,080
appear at twenty billion, so we submit the strong anneal.

50
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:31,962
All benchmarks are zero-shot, on the full

51
00:03:31,962 --> 00:03:35,055
evaluation sets, with lm-evaluation-harness.

52
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:39,655
Now the model itself, running locally on a laptop.

53
00:03:39,655 --> 00:03:42,830
We give it three prompts and let it continue.

54
00:03:42,830 --> 00:03:47,105
The text is grammatical and keeps the register of the prompt.

55
00:03:47,105 --> 00:03:50,652
It also gets facts wrong and repeats itself, which is

56
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:53,530
what we expect at fifty million parameters.

57
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:01,540
Everything needed to reproduce this is in the repository. uv installs the pinned

58
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:06,125
environment, and the Modal scripts build the data, train and evaluate.

59
00:04:06,125 --> 00:04:11,925
On AI use: this project was built with Claude Code agents under human direction.

60
00:04:11,925 --> 00:04:16,675
The agents wrote the code, ran the experiments and drafted the docs.

61
00:04:16,675 --> 00:04:19,849
Mohammed Alibhai set the goals, made the decisions,

62
00:04:19,849 --> 00:04:22,525
reviewed the work and paid for the compute.

63
00:04:22,525 --> 00:04:24,450
Thanks for watching.

